were homogeneous Poisson
process they get the blue line.

00:20:55.101 --> 00:20:57.160
The red is an inhomogeneous
Poisson process

00:20:57.161 --> 00:20:58.830
where I've tried
to learn the time

00:20:58.831 --> 00:21:00.370
of day intensity and I

00:21:00.371 --> 00:21:03.230
barely get any uplift
at all from doing that.

00:21:03.231 --> 00:21:06.360
Whereas, the self-exciting
models do particularly well.

00:21:06.361 --> 00:21:08.220
This is why the black line
is the bold one with

00:21:08.221 --> 00:21:10.260
the non-parametric intensity
and that's one,

00:21:10.261 --> 00:21:11.990
which doing best, which
is why we favored it.

00:21:11.991 --> 00:21:13.420
So, I think it's

00:21:13.421 --> 00:21:14.350
incredible for homo- I

00:21:14.351 --> 00:21:15.630
mean it's not
my work, it's Matt's.

00:21:15.631 --> 00:21:17.280
So, I can say that I think so

00:21:17.281 --> 00:21:19.480
I believe we can
fit that so well.

00:21:19.481 --> 00:21:23.195
So, just predicting when
the next event is going to be.

00:21:23.196 --> 00:21:26.650
So, yeah, that's
interesting stuff.

00:21:27.000 --> 00:21:29.990
So, that's edges dealt with.

00:21:30.390 --> 00:21:32.590
Not perfectly covered, but that's

00:21:32.591 --> 00:21:34.235
all we've done on edges.

00:21:34.236 --> 00:21:36.335
So, next up nodes.

00:21:36.336 --> 00:21:42.870
One level higher up in the
sense of black granularity.

00:21:42.871 --> 00:21:48.170
So, we've done some modeling
of destinations of nodes.

00:21:48.171 --> 00:21:51.820
So, looking at the sequence
of IP addresses, say,

00:21:51.821 --> 00:21:54.070
a computer connects to-

00:21:54.071 --> 00:21:56.800
really relate to some work
I did with Josh on

00:21:56.801 --> 00:21:58.800
port scoring a long time ago,

00:21:58.801 --> 00:22:00.940
so it's the same model again.

00:22:00.941 --> 00:22:05.885
So here, we think about
scoring every single events.

00:22:05.886 --> 00:22:07.740
So look at a node and see
who it connects to over

00:22:07.741 --> 00:22:10.570
time and scoring the surprise
of every single attempt,

00:22:10.571 --> 00:22:11.640
"Oh, we've connected
to that one" and

00:22:11.641 --> 00:22:13.805
so on, so really simple things.

00:22:13.806 --> 00:22:16.000
Then aggregate the surprise using

00:22:16.001 --> 00:22:17.770
control charts or
p-value combiners.

00:22:17.771 --> 00:22:19.940
I'm big into p-value
combiners these days,

00:22:19.941 --> 00:22:21.400
which is quite odd as a Bayesian,

00:22:21.401 --> 00:22:23.320
but I call at them anyway.

00:22:23.321 --> 00:22:26.510
So, in 2016, we've
got a paper where we

00:22:26.511 --> 00:22:29.040
looked at modeling from
a server-based perspective.

00:22:29.041 --> 00:22:32.065
So, I look at a server and
it has its own model of,

00:22:32.066 --> 00:22:33.320
"Okay, who's going to
connect to me next?

00:22:33.321 --> 00:22:34.590
I have a probability
mass function

00:22:34.591 --> 00:22:35.870
on the go of who I think,

00:22:35.871 --> 00:22:37.130
we should learn over time

00:22:37.131 --> 00:22:38.820
who I think is going to
connect to the next."

00:22:38.821 --> 00:22:40.640
So, we look at the sequence
of clients, x1,

00:22:40.641 --> 00:22:43.185
x2, and so on which
connect to a server y.

00:22:43.186 --> 00:22:44.860
When you simply model
as a multinomial,

00:22:44.861 --> 00:22:46.815
we saw nothing clever
going on at all.

00:22:46.816 --> 00:22:48.260
With an unbounded number of

00:22:48.261 --> 00:22:50.230
categories that makes it
a bit more interesting.

00:22:50.231 --> 00:22:51.510
So, imagine I don't know

00:22:51.511 --> 00:22:53.930
how the limits of my
computer network,

00:22:53.931 --> 00:22:55.420
there could be
arbitrarily many computers

00:22:55.421 --> 00:22:56.820
that don't work, so
I want to split it,

00:22:56.821 --> 00:22:58.345
so I could think about
having a base measure

00:22:58.346 --> 00:22:59.730
on the names of my computers.

00:22:59.731 --> 00:23:01.400
So, I think it's
quite a fun thought and

00:23:01.401 --> 00:23:04.350
some measure of
confidence in that alpha.

00:23:04.351 --> 00:23:06.870
Then from that, just from
that simple Dirichlet

00:23:06.871 --> 00:23:09.145
process model with a multinomial,

00:23:09.146 --> 00:23:12.515
we get a p-value for
every single observation.

00:23:12.516 --> 00:23:14.945
So, you can think of alpha
x star is the measure

00:23:14.946 --> 00:23:18.660
attached to observing
the next event being on x.

00:23:18.661 --> 00:23:20.900
So, you get a chunk
of probability from

00:23:20.901 --> 00:23:23.240
the base measure, and
then essentially,

00:23:23.241 --> 00:23:24.320
it's a glorified histogram

00:23:24.321 --> 00:23:25.795
accounts of how many
times I've seen

00:23:25.796 --> 00:23:29.780
this client connect before
along with the base measure.

00:23:29.781 --> 00:23:32.650
So, really simple things.

00:23:35.060 --> 00:23:38.745
So, to score an edge,
first of all,

00:23:38.746 --> 00:23:40.955
we just look at all the
connections from

00:23:40.956 --> 00:23:43.410
each unique client to the server

00:23:43.411 --> 00:23:46.300
y and found the minimum
p-value of that.

00:23:46.301 --> 00:23:47.760
That should follow
these distribution

00:23:47.761 --> 00:23:49.479
up to some approximations,

00:23:49.480 --> 00:23:51.950
at least assuming independence
and continuity and so on

00:23:51.951 --> 00:23:56.570
and being in for naught one
and naught the discrete.

00:23:57.430 --> 00:23:59.760
We transform those to p-values,

00:23:59.761 --> 00:24:03.380
we get a p-value for every edge.

00:24:03.381 --> 00:24:05.070
So then, from a node perspective,

00:24:05.071 --> 00:24:06.920
what would you do is
then combine all those

00:24:06.921 --> 00:24:08.920
for a local and clients
I want to say,

00:24:08.921 --> 00:24:11.295
"Okay, what was the p-value

00:24:11.296 --> 00:24:13.075
I got for each of your edges?"

00:24:13.076 --> 00:24:14.830
So for a computer here,

00:24:14.831 --> 00:24:15.850
which turned out to where

00:24:15.851 --> 00:24:17.010
the more interesting
ones is this from

00:24:17.011 --> 00:24:20.405
the Los Alamos network data.

00:24:20.406 --> 00:24:22.525
This is the p-values we got for

00:24:22.526 --> 00:24:24.740
all its edges so loads
of surprise going on.

00:24:24.741 --> 00:24:25.820
So, this was one of the red team

00:24:25.821 --> 00:24:28.330
attacked nodes, this guy here.

00:24:28.331 --> 00:24:31.710
So, it was really easy to
find, those data are so easy.

00:24:31.711 --> 00:24:34.060
There's no great glory
in winning this,

00:24:34.061 --> 00:24:35.620
but it's just that
a nice demonstration

00:24:35.621 --> 00:24:37.340
of how we can do
simple models and get

00:24:37.341 --> 00:24:39.670
well-calibrated
decent answers out

00:24:39.671 --> 00:24:42.165
in an automated fashion.

00:24:42.166 --> 00:24:44.250
So, yeah, we use
Fisher's method to

00:24:44.251 --> 00:24:46.295
combine to get
a node's score in the end.

00:24:46.296 --> 00:24:48.190
Those are fairly
arbitrary choices of

00:24:48.191 --> 00:24:49.930
combiners so a lot of
my work since then has been

00:24:49.931 --> 00:24:51.330
looking at how you choose

00:24:51.331 --> 00:24:54.030
a proper p and make a proper
choice of p-value combiners.

00:24:54.031 --> 00:24:56.030
So, I've done a paper on that.

00:24:56.031 --> 00:24:59.175
&gt;&gt; Is that paper
published yet or?

00:24:59.176 --> 00:25:01.875
&gt;&gt; It is, it went to
Biometrika this year.

00:25:01.876 --> 00:25:03.430
&gt;&gt; Excellent.

00:25:07.050 --> 00:25:10.150
&gt;&gt; So, more interesting
is- that's okay,

00:25:10.151 --> 00:25:12.980
that's fine I can look at
just my surprise of going

00:25:12.981 --> 00:25:15.960
to edges that I've been to
before or not and so on,

00:25:15.961 --> 00:25:18.060
but really we're most
interested in new edges.

00:25:18.061 --> 00:25:19.480
So, if a node form new edges,

00:25:19.481 --> 00:25:22.545
I want to think how
surprising that is, okay?

00:25:22.546 --> 00:25:26.610
So, yeah, in the red team
attacking the line all data,

00:25:26.611 --> 00:25:28.490
there was a lot of
whole bursts of

00:25:28.491 --> 00:25:31.050
new edge activity
created by the red team,

00:25:31.051 --> 00:25:32.605
and that should be an easy way of

00:25:32.606 --> 00:25:34.485
detecting unusual behavior.

00:25:34.486 --> 00:25:39.620
So, what we want to do
is to understand whether

00:25:39.621 --> 00:25:42.470
new edge activity is odd
for that host because

00:25:42.471 --> 00:25:43.900
the point is that different hosts

00:25:43.901 --> 00:25:45.515
form new edges at
very different rates.

00:25:45.516 --> 00:25:47.300
Some make lots of new edges
and some are much

00:25:47.301 --> 00:25:50.640
more consistent in
the nodes that they visit.

00:25:50.641 --> 00:25:54.050
So, there are different
rates between hosts.

00:25:54.051 --> 00:25:56.185
Also, we want to think about

00:25:56.186 --> 00:25:58.260
who the edges being formed with.

00:25:58.261 --> 00:26:00.060
If it's a new edge, okay, who to?

00:26:00.061 --> 00:26:03.610
Is that the node you ought
to be connecting to?

00:26:03.611 --> 00:26:05.830
Or was it a node someone else
should be connecting to?

00:26:05.831 --> 00:26:07.870
So, we want to understand what's

00:26:07.871 --> 00:26:09.570
the right node for you
to be connecting to,

00:26:09.571 --> 00:26:10.730
as well as understand the rate to

00:26:10.731 --> 00:26:12.135
which you should be
forming those things.

00:26:12.136 --> 00:26:15.485
So we were looking at building
models of these things.

00:26:15.486 --> 00:26:18.350
So again, we look at
this as accounting.

00:26:18.351 --> 00:26:19.540
As we look at
the counting process of

00:26:19.541 --> 00:26:21.870
new edges being formed.

00:26:22.260 --> 00:26:26.830
So, if capital X of
my sources are my clients,

00:26:26.831 --> 00:26:29.845
and capital Y are my
servers and destinations,

00:26:29.846 --> 00:26:33.155
we want to model
the new edge intensity

00:26:33.156 --> 00:26:38.290
lambda x y of t for
client-server pair x y.

00:26:38.430 --> 00:26:42.470
So, it's a new edge intensity.

00:26:42.471 --> 00:26:43.570
So, if G_t is

00:26:43.571 --> 00:26:44.760
my current graph at time

00:26:44.761 --> 00:26:46.225
t of all the edges
I've ever seen,

00:26:46.226 --> 00:26:49.030
that I've got
indicator saying if I

00:26:49.031 --> 00:26:51.785
need it to be inside and
need not to be in G_t,

00:26:51.786 --> 00:26:53.630
so it's in x cross y slash

00:26:53.631 --> 00:26:55.560
G_t so it needs to
have not been seen.

00:26:55.561 --> 00:26:57.430
Otherwise, this indicates
there as a zero,

00:26:57.431 --> 00:26:58.850
got no intensity of a new age

00:26:58.851 --> 00:27:00.360
there if I've already seen it.

00:27:00.361 --> 00:27:04.300
So, that's all that first bit
is saying. It makes sense?

00:27:04.301 --> 00:27:05.965
Then, there'll be some

00:27:05.966 --> 00:27:07.640
seasonality function which
I'm going to treat as

00:27:07.641 --> 00:27:09.390
a nuisance parameter
and not do inference

00:27:09.391 --> 00:27:11.350
on that recurring theme in this.

00:27:11.351 --> 00:27:14.580
So, we're just not doing
that much seasonality modeling,

00:27:14.581 --> 00:27:17.175
which is very early,
but that's all.

00:27:17.176 --> 00:27:18.790
Then some other covariates,

00:27:18.791 --> 00:27:20.850
which had to do with the degree,

00:27:20.851 --> 00:27:22.740
the outdegree of x,
the indegree of y.

00:27:22.741 --> 00:27:24.940
So how much they make new edges?

00:27:24.941 --> 00:27:26.930
Whether they're in
a burst of activity?

00:27:26.931 --> 00:27:28.570
So, this is an indicator
is equal to one,

00:27:28.571 --> 00:27:31.120
if my last edge I met
if it was a new one.

00:27:31.121 --> 00:27:32.700
This is equal to one if

00:27:32.701 --> 00:27:34.110
the last two edges
that connects on

00:27:34.111 --> 00:27:36.620
my new- further terms

00:27:36.621 --> 00:27:38.150
didn't make any difference
in stuff we did.

00:27:38.151 --> 00:27:39.980
So, those tend to be
new because covariants,

00:27:39.981 --> 00:27:41.710
whether or not I'm in
a burst of making new edges,

00:27:41.711 --> 00:27:42.530
that's a good
prediction whether I'm

00:27:42.531 --> 00:27:43.960
about to make another.

00:27:43.961 --> 00:27:46.300
Plus, some mysterious last

00:27:46.301 --> 00:27:48.385
final term of
some latent variable,

00:27:48.386 --> 00:27:49.845
which is going to characterize

00:27:49.846 --> 00:27:51.760
the attraction Z_xy
we're going to kept

00:27:51.761 --> 00:27:53.300
characterized
the attraction from x to

00:27:53.301 --> 00:27:56.190
y in some model that
we're going to build.

00:27:56.191 --> 00:27:58.810
So, we had two versions
of doing that.

00:27:58.811 --> 00:28:00.930
One is a hard thresholding
clustering model,

00:28:00.931 --> 00:28:02.345
so we cluster clients
and servers and

00:28:02.346 --> 00:28:04.185
have a bit stochastic
block models, and say,

00:28:04.186 --> 00:28:06.860
this type of client connects
to this group of servers,

00:28:06.861 --> 00:28:08.150
that's a good fit.

00:28:08.151 --> 00:28:09.170
So we cluster up

00:28:09.171 --> 00:28:10.885
the clients and servers
into blocks and say,

00:28:10.886 --> 00:28:11.840
"Okay, well that block does

00:28:11.841 --> 00:28:12.940
tend to connect to that block.

00:28:12.941 --> 00:28:14.090
So, you are in then that's

00:28:14.091 --> 00:28:16.050
okay or that's some more unusual.

00:28:16.051 --> 00:28:17.890
Or we do something
more soft and do

00:28:17.891 --> 00:28:19.800
a latent feature model with

00:28:19.801 --> 00:28:21.330
an Indian buffet process prior.

00:28:21.331 --> 00:28:22.135
So, that's we're trying to do

00:28:22.136 --> 00:28:23.345
a much more flexible style of

00:28:23.346 --> 00:28:26.130
modeling that actually
turns out to do better.

00:28:26.131 --> 00:28:28.555
So, that's a paper which is
under review at the moment.

00:28:28.556 --> 00:28:30.940
It's what we've done,
but it's not over yet.

00:28:30.941 --> 00:28:32.380
But yeah, that
turns out to be the

00:28:32.381 --> 00:28:34.125
best thing for what we did.

00:28:34.126 --> 00:28:40.840
Okay. Here's a picture just
to illustrate that in action.

00:28:40.841 --> 00:28:42.385
So these are the p-values

00:28:42.386 --> 00:28:43.510
that we obtained for
two communities.

00:28:43.511 --> 00:28:44.595
This is from the brought back,

00:28:44.596 --> 00:28:46.410
same infected one again
looking at now from

00:28:46.411 --> 00:28:49.695
a new perspective and just
some other random-ish.

00:28:49.696 --> 00:28:53.990
An infected computer,
or less anomalous.

00:28:53.991 --> 00:28:56.880
So, these are
control charts that use

00:28:56.881 --> 00:29:00.910
a combined p-value
functions over time.

00:29:00.911 --> 00:29:02.640
Here are various
p-value thresholds

00:29:02.641 --> 00:29:03.840
drawn on with the workup by

00:29:03.841 --> 00:29:05.780
Monte-Carlo what they
should be to calibrate

00:29:05.781 --> 00:29:06.860
these things because somehow more

00:29:06.861 --> 00:29:08.050
events than others and so on.

00:29:08.051 --> 00:29:10.605
But on a p-value scale,
these are now the same.

00:29:10.606 --> 00:29:13.730
You can see that here
we go way down in

00:29:13.731 --> 00:29:16.960
low p-values in terms of
the new edges we're making here.

00:29:16.961 --> 00:29:18.655
Whereas, for the uninfected one,

00:29:18.656 --> 00:29:20.915
it makes new edges,
but they're okay.

00:29:20.916 --> 00:29:22.455
They're not surprising either by

00:29:22.456 --> 00:29:24.635
rate or by who they're
with and so on.

00:29:24.636 --> 00:29:27.350
So, that's it in action.

00:29:28.020 --> 00:29:30.950
&gt;&gt; To make the,

00:29:31.260 --> 00:29:33.370
is this the model adaptive?

00:29:33.371 --> 00:29:36.860
Is it learning over time or?

00:29:37.050 --> 00:29:39.310
&gt;&gt; Of course, I think so.

00:29:39.311 --> 00:29:43.840
I've read in it as
a time-varying covariate.

00:29:43.841 --> 00:29:45.480
So, I think why we did it

00:29:45.481 --> 00:29:47.170
was we have to read
data in chunks,

00:29:47.171 --> 00:29:48.590
and update the model over time,

00:29:48.591 --> 00:29:50.470
and it seems to work out easily.

00:29:50.471 --> 00:29:51.770
Reading all the data is a bit

00:29:51.771 --> 00:29:53.310
problematic for us in
fitting this model,

00:29:53.311 --> 00:29:54.370
so we tend to just take

00:29:54.371 --> 00:29:56.640
a subset and just
learning becomes smooth.

00:29:56.641 --> 00:30:00.090
So, I think so.

00:30:01.750 --> 00:30:05.630
Silvia Metelli had done
all the hard work for that,

00:30:05.631 --> 00:30:06.830
so she should be
the person to actually

00:30:06.831 --> 00:30:08.465
say, "No, it's not.

00:30:08.466 --> 00:30:11.400
Yes, it is." Or it could be you.

00:30:14.250 --> 00:30:17.495
So, that's quite a nice general,

00:30:17.496 --> 00:30:20.260
this sort of new edges
or unlikely edges for

00:30:20.261 --> 00:30:22.340
some reason is
a nice general concept.

00:30:22.341 --> 00:30:23.290
We can look at different things.

00:30:23.291 --> 00:30:24.730
We could surprise the sequence

00:30:24.731 --> 00:30:26.810
of processes executed
by a computer,

00:30:26.811 --> 00:30:30.460
and measure the accumulates
surprise, and also,

00:30:30.461 --> 00:30:33.490
a data fusion combining
host processes and

00:30:33.491 --> 00:30:34.730
network level things into

00:30:34.731 --> 00:30:37.190
getting scores for
each of those things,

00:30:37.191 --> 00:30:39.020
that's kind of what
we're doing under

00:30:39.021 --> 00:30:42.180
the data-centric
engineering program ATI.

00:30:42.530 --> 00:30:44.670
With Josh, in the past,

00:30:44.671 --> 00:30:46.935
we've looked at port scoring.

00:30:46.936 --> 00:30:51.470
Modelling how unlikely using
different services is say,

00:30:51.471 --> 00:30:55.050
a lots of applications of
such simple models inside.

00:30:56.750 --> 00:31:00.935
Then, finally, a Whole Graph

00:31:00.936 --> 00:31:02.500
perspective might do for time.

00:31:02.501 --> 00:31:09.105
So, this is the value

00:31:09.106 --> 00:31:10.800
for the highest level we
can think about taking.

00:31:10.801 --> 00:31:16.795
So, this is joint work
with Pat and Francesco.

00:31:16.796 --> 00:31:20.410
So, here, we want to look at
the full graph and look at

00:31:20.411 --> 00:31:23.775
how we can learn underlying
structure in the graph.

00:31:23.776 --> 00:31:26.770
So, we're going to consider
a binary adjacency matrix

00:31:26.771 --> 00:31:28.480
are not perfect about
counts just yet,

00:31:28.481 --> 00:31:30.600
think about the binary
directed adjacency

00:31:30.601 --> 00:31:33.270
matrix At for my entire network.

00:31:33.271 --> 00:31:36.850
Such that (Atij is equal to one,

00:31:36.851 --> 00:31:39.090
if and only if i
is connected to j,

00:31:39.091 --> 00:31:42.070
which will run that
way by time t. So,

00:31:42.071 --> 00:31:43.720
here is just a
cartoon graph of what

00:31:43.721 --> 00:31:46.125
